BUG 7 — a SHA under a NESTED `base`/`base_ref` key is in NEITHER tally
  => `coverage: FULL` + `unexamined=0` + `stale=0` + exit 0 over a genuinely orphaned commit.
  This is the C15 false green, reachable again through a different key name, in the
  cycle that shipped C15's fix.

WHAT THE GUARD CLAIMS (base-descent-check.sh, fix-cycle-3 tip 5442b06d)

  L288-291 (NON_EV_EXAMINED):
    # Keys this guard examines OUTSIDE I-2's harvest — I-1's and I-3's operands. A SHA
    # under one of these is not "unexamined" merely because it is not in the I-2 allow
    # list, so the unexamined tally must not count it.

  L542-544 (the unexamined block):
    # Same keying rule as the harvest, so a SHA is either harvested or counted here,
    # never neither (that gap was BUG 5).

WHY BOTH ARE FALSE
  Both jq blocks key on the SHA's NEAREST STRING ANCESTOR and match the name at ANY depth.
  I-1 and I-3, however, read only the TOP-LEVEL fields (`rf '.base'`, `rf '.base_ref'`).
  So `phases.9b.base` is excluded from the unexamined tally on the grounds that "the guard
  examines it" — and nothing examines it. It is not harvested (base is not on EV_FIELDS),
  not asserted by I-1/I-3, and not counted as skipped. Neither tally. Exactly BUG 5's shape.

LIVE REPRODUCTION (hermetic, throwaway repo)
  fixture: base B, branch feat/X, commit C1, base advances to B', rebase -> C1'
           run file records base=B, base_ref=main, phases.6.commit=C1' (live),
           and phases.9b.base = C1 (the ORPHANED pre-rebase commit)
  orphan SHA planted at phases.9b.base = 0a324b6 (unreachable from HEAD: ORPHAN)

  $ bash base-descent-check.sh --key NEARS-NB --worktree <fixture> --runs-dir <fixture>/tasks/runs --phase 9b
    I-1 DESCENT   PASS  (1 assertion(s))
    I-3 CURRENCY  PASS  (1 assertion(s))
    I-2 EVIDENCE  PASS  (1 assertion(s))
  -- result: PASS — assertions_made=3 (I-1 1, I-2 1, I-3 1); passed=3 failed=0 stale=0
     not_asserted=0 unresolvable=0 unexamined=0; harvest=1 sha(s) from 1/35 curated key
     name(s); coverage: FULL (I-2 harvest is an allow list of 35 key names, so it is
     PARTIAL by construction); exit=0 --
  exit=0

  Every machine-readable signal is clean, `coverage` reads FULL, and no UNEXAMINED line
  prints — with an orphaned commit sitting in the file. That is the exact sentence C15's
  own test-file comment uses to describe the defect it was written to close.

CORPUS REACHABILITY (measured 2026-08-11, /Users/Apple/Projects/nears/tasks/runs, 905 files)
  93 SHA-shaped values sit under a nested `base`/`base_ref` key, across 82 files
  (70 NEARS-*, 10 AP-*, 2 other). Shapes seen: phases.<n>.base, phases.9a.shas.base,
  phases.m2.base, commit.base, final_status.base, phases.9b.base.
  MITIGATING, stated honestly: all 80 resolvable NEARS-* instances are currently ancestors
  of the primary HEAD, so there is NO live orphan hiding in the corpus today. The channel
  is latent and demonstrable, not actively firing.

MINIMAL HONEST FIX (either is sufficient — this is a labelling defect, not a harvest one)
  (a) scope NON_EV_EXAMINED to TOP-LEVEL `base`/`base_ref` only (`($p|length)==1`), so a
      nested one falls into `unexamined` and breaks `coverage: FULL`; or
  (b) DELETE both sentences above per the cycle-3 ruling and state the blindness plainly:
      "a SHA under a `base`/`base_ref` key at any depth is excluded from this tally, and
      only the TOP-LEVEL two are actually examined — a nested one is in neither number."
